WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.769
I'm in Weldon Spring,
near St Louis, Missouri, USA,

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.780
and in the 1950s and 60s,

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.380
this strange, grey, wind-blasted hill
wasn't here:

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.700
instead, there was a uranium processing factory.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.950
And conditions for the workers back then were
shocking by today's standards.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.029
It wasn't quite the 1940s,

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:21.690
you can find testimony from back then of workers
hand-scooping uranium ore,

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.080
but there are tales of
spilled radioactive powder,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.840
of overflowing disposal pits,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.019
and of strange-looking frogs.

00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:32.169
And I am not making that up.

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:36.000
When the factory here was running, it was
known as "The Clean One".

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.430
And, well, it wasn't.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.320
The plant here closed in 1966,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.329
as the most contaminated site in the area:

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:46.670
PCBs, mercury, asbestos, and radioactive waste.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.980
In the 1980s, the Environmental Protection
Agency declared Weldon Spring

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.540
a Superfund Site,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.080
meaning it would finally be cleaned up.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.430
And the result is this: a seven-storey high
nuclear waste dump,

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.469
the permanent disposal cell,

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:04.479
containing more than a million cubic metres
of toxic waste,

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:08.850
and hopefully keeping the local residents
safe for the next 1,000 years or so.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.259
And that was going to be pretty much all of
the video.

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:14.509
If you search this place online,
that's as far as a lot of articles go.

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:15.509
Except.

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:17.450
Then I did a bit more research.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:21.490
And I found out about Coldwater Creek,
a few miles away,

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.490
where radioactive waste from
the Manhattan Project,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.979
the project that built the first nuclear bombs,

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:28.270
the waste was just dumped there
in the 1940s,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:29.340
with the government's approval.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.429
That waste eventually flowed, decades later,
into people's backyards after flooding.

00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:36.139
That was in 2015.

00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:38.979
Then there's the West Lake Landfill,
on the other side of St Louis,

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:41.609
where an underground trash fire has been burning

00:01:41.609 --> 00:01:46.429
very close to another radioactive waste site
for seven years.

00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:49.560
Now, the risk is apparently minor.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.319
No-one is about to find a glowing barrel in
their backyard.

00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:55.929
But radiation hazards are invisible and terrifying:

00:01:55.929 --> 00:01:59.430
local residents are concerned about
clusters of cancer diagnoses,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.429
about infant mortality
caused by contaminated groundwater.

00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:06.569
There are wells for drinking water
only a mile away.

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:08.709
The state department of health investigated

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:11.460
and their results set no-one's mind at ease.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.709
There are so many confounding factors

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:19.430
that the facts are, well, somewhere between
confusing and unknown,

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.310
and people are scared.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.340
This site is a legacy of a time when
the people in charge

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.420
didn't know, or didn't care, about the risks
of radioactive contamination.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.349
But this isn't a monument to how we've fixed
their mistakes:

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:36.091
it's a reminder that we're still fixing them,

00:02:36.091 --> 00:02:40.629
and that we'll still need to for centuries
or millennia to come.

